 ## Overview
-The Type-1 Direct Connection Algorithm computes a refractive light path between a vertex $\mathbf{x}_0$ inside a heterogeneous medium and a vertex $\mathbf{y}$ located on the medium's boundary [S1]. It solves the boundary value problem (BVP) of finding a curve satisfying Hamilton’s equations that connects these two points by optimizing the initial velocity $\mathbf{v}_0$ of a ray propagated from $\mathbf{x}_0$ [S1]. It is distinguished from Type-2 connections by utilizing the implicit surface constraint of the boundary, which provides a better-behaved loss function and faster convergence for surface intersections [S1].
+The Type-1 Direct Connection Algorithm computes a refractive light path between a vertex $\mathbf{y}$ located on a medium boundary (defined by an implicit surface $g(\cdot)=0$) and a vertex $\mathbf{x}_0$ located inside the medium [S1]. It solves the boundary value problem by optimizing the initial velocity $\mathbf{v}_0$ of a ray propagated from $\mathbf{x}_0$ such that the ray intersects $\mathbf{y}$ [S1]. It is distinguished from Type-2 connections by its use of the explicit geometric constraint of the medium boundary, which provides a better-behaved loss function and faster convergence rates for surface intersections [S1].
 
